**Key idea:** keep two running values. A new maximum pushes the old maximum down to second place; a value strictly between them only replaces second. Values equal to the max are skipped so duplicates don't count as "second". O(n) time, O(1) space.

| x | first | second | action |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | 3 | none | first empty |
| 7 | 7 | 3 | x > first: shift |
| 7 | 7 | 3 | x == first: skip |
| 5 | 7 | 5 | between: update second |
| 9 | 9 | 7 | x > first: shift |
| 1 | 9 | 7 | smaller: nothing |

### Solution
- `std::optional` avoids the classic `INT_MIN` sentinel bug (an array that actually contains `INT_MIN`).
- Pass the vector by `const&`: no copy, no modification.

### Code
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <optional>
#include <utility>
#include <cstdio>
using namespace std;

// {max, second distinct max}; either may be empty
pair<optional<int>, optional<int>> top_two(const vector<int>& a) {
    optional<int> first, second;
    for (int x : a) {
        if (!first || x > *first) {          // new maximum
            second = first;
            first  = x;
        } else if (x < *first && (!second || x > *second)) {
            second = x;                      // strictly between
        }
    }
    return {first, second};
}

int main() {
    auto [f, s] = top_two({3, 7, 7, 5, 9, 1});
    printf("%d %d\n", *f, *s);               // 9 7
}
```

