### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Empty input, single element, all equal: second must be "none".
- Using `x >= first` would make second equal to first on duplicates.

### Follow-ups
- **Kth largest?** Min-heap of size k (`priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>>`), O(n log k); or `nth_element`, O(n) average.
- **Third maximum (LeetCode 414)?** Same pattern with three slots.

**Key idea:** two pointers on one array. The **read** pointer `i` scans everything; the **write** pointer `k` marks the end of the unique prefix. Because the array is sorted, a value is new exactly when it differs from its left neighbor.

| i | a[i] | new? | write | k after |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 1 | no (== 1) | | 1 |
| 2 | 2 | yes | a[1] = 2 | 2 |
| 3 | 3 | yes | a[2] = 3 | 3 |
| 4 | 3 | no | | 3 |
| 5 | 3 | no | | 3 |
| 6 | 4 | yes | a[3] = 4 | 4 |

Result: `k = 4`, prefix `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.

