### Follow-ups
- **Why not `erase` inside the loop?** Each erase is O(n), making it O(n^2).

**Key idea:** in general, **count** with a hash map and scan again in input order. When every other value appears exactly **twice**, XOR all elements: pairs cancel (`x ^ x = 0`) and the single value remains (`x ^ 0 = x`).

XOR trace for `[4, 1, 2, 1, 2]`:

| step | x | r (binary) | r |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| start | | 000 | 0 |
| 1 | 4 | 100 | 4 |
| 2 | 1 | 101 | 5 |
| 3 | 2 | 111 | 7 |
| 4 | 1 | 110 | 6 |
| 5 | 2 | 100 | **4** |

### Code
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <utility>
using namespace std;

// General: first element with count 1, O(n) time / O(n) space
int find_unique(const vector<int>& a) {
    unordered_map<int, int> cnt;
    for (int x : a) cnt[x]++;
    for (int x : a) if (cnt[x] == 1) return x;   // input order
    return -1;                                   // or std::optional<int>
}

// Every other value appears exactly twice: O(n) / O(1)
int find_unique_xor(const vector<int>& a) {
    int r = 0;
    for (int x : a) r ^= x;
    return r;
}

// Follow-up: exactly two unique values, others twice (LeetCode 260)
pair<int, int> two_unique(const vector<int>& a) {
    unsigned x = 0;
    for (int v : a) x ^= (unsigned)v;            // x = p ^ q, non-zero
    unsigned low = x & (~x + 1);                 // a bit where p and q differ
    int p = 0, q = 0;
    for (int v : a) {
        if ((unsigned)v & low) p ^= v;
        else                   q ^= v;
    }
    return {p, q};
}
```

