- **And in SystemVerilog?** Very different: an SV `struct` is a value type (packed or unpacked, copied on assignment, no methods), while an SV `class` is a dynamically allocated object accessed through a handle.

**Key idea:** a `virtual` member function is dispatched at **runtime** based on the object's dynamic type, through a per-class table of function pointers (the **vtable**). A call through a base pointer/reference runs the derived override.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  P["Txn* t (base pointer)"] --> OBJ["AxiTxn object: vptr"]
  OBJ --> VT["AxiTxn vtable"]
  VT --> F["AxiTxn::print"]
```

```cpp
class Txn {
public:
    virtual ~Txn() = default;
    virtual void print() const { printf("base txn\n"); }
};
class AxiTxn : public Txn {
public:
    uint32_t addr = 0;
    void print() const override { printf("axi addr=0x%x\n", addr); }
};

unique_ptr<Txn> t = make_unique<AxiTxn>();
t->print();                 // "axi addr=0x0": dynamic dispatch
```

| | non-virtual | virtual |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Binding | compile time (static type) | runtime (dynamic type) |
| Cost | direct call | one indirect call via vptr |
| Through base pointer | base version | derived override |

