**Key idea:** `virtual void f() = 0;` declares an interface with no implementation. A class with at least one pure virtual is **abstract**: it can't be instantiated, only derived from, and every concrete subclass must implement the function.

```cpp
class Checker {                              // abstract
public:
    virtual ~Checker() = default;
    virtual bool check(uint32_t exp, uint32_t act) = 0;
};
class ExactChecker : public Checker {
public:
    bool check(uint32_t e, uint32_t a) override { return e == a; }
};
// Checker c;            // error: abstract
ExactChecker ok;         // fine
```

| | C++ | SystemVerilog |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Abstract class | has a pure virtual | `virtual class base;` |
| Pure method | `virtual void f() = 0;` | `pure virtual function void f();` |
| Instantiate abstract | compile error | compile error |

