**Key idea:** `delete base_ptr` calls the destructor of the **static** type unless the destructor is `virtual`. Without it, the derived part is never destroyed: resources leak and the behavior is undefined.

```cpp
struct Base { ~Base() { puts("~Base"); } };              // NOT virtual
struct Derived : Base {
    int* buf = new int[1024];
    ~Derived() { delete[] buf; puts("~Derived"); }
};

Base* p = new Derived;
delete p;                     // prints only "~Base": buf leaks, UB
```

| Base destructor | `delete base_ptr` runs | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| non-virtual | `~Base` only | leak / UB |
| `virtual ~Base()` | `~Derived` then `~Base` | correct |

```cpp
struct Base { virtual ~Base() = default; };   // the rule: any class with virtual functions
```

