**Key idea:** the compiler-generated copy copies **member values**; for a raw pointer member that means both objects point to the **same** buffer (shallow). Destroying both deletes it twice. A **deep** copy allocates a new buffer and copies the contents.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph Shallow
    A1["a.data"] --> BUF1["heap buffer"]
    B1["b.data (copy)"] --> BUF1
  end
  subgraph Deep
    A2["a.data"] --> BUF2["buffer 1"]
    B2["b.data (copy)"] --> BUF3["buffer 2 (copied contents)"]
  end
```

```cpp
struct Buf {
    int* data; size_t n;
    Buf(size_t n) : data(new int[n]), n(n) {}
    ~Buf() { delete[] data; }
    // Without these, copies share 'data' -> double delete
    Buf(const Buf& o) : data(new int[o.n]), n(o.n) { std::copy(o.data, o.data + n, data); }
    Buf& operator=(const Buf& o) = delete;     // or implement it too
};
// Best: struct Buf { std::vector<int> data; };  // rule of zero: copies are deep automatically
```

| Rule | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Rule of three | if you write a destructor, copy ctor or copy assignment, you need all three |
| Rule of five | plus move ctor and move assignment |
| Rule of zero | use RAII members (`vector`, `unique_ptr`) and write none |

